#!/bin/sh
# script/icount: the instruction-count instrument (milestone 78).
#
#   script/icount                    both ISAs
#   script/icount --arch aarch64     one leg
#   script/icount --arch riscv64
#
# Boots a `--features icount` kernel under `-icount shift=0,sleep=off`, where QEMU's virtual clock
# advances by exactly one nanosecond per guest instruction retired and by nothing else. The guest
# asserts two claims that no wall-clock test on either ISA can make, because from inside the guest a
# slow handler and a descheduled emulator are the same observation:
#
#   1. the timer fired at the deadline the kernel armed (on riscv64, that SBI was armed with the
#      DEADLINE word rather than with something else that keeps the array looking right), and
#   2. the handler takes fewer than N instructions, deadline to re-armed.
#
# It also asserts zero missed ticks, which is only assertable here: the miss taxonomy on both ISAs
# exists to tell a slow handler from a descheduled emulator, and virtual time has no deschedules.
#
# **Not part of script/test, deliberately**, and the reason is not that it is slow: an identical
# compute-bound boot measured the same either way. It is that all vCPUs share ONE virtual clock under
# -icount, so the instrument is single-hart and a suite run here would stop proving every cross-core
# property it exists for; and that a clock-bound wait costs guest instructions rather than host time.
# This is its own boot for the same reason script/bench is. See
# design/roadmap/0078-load-sensitive-assertions.md and notes/instruction-clock.md.
#
# Name: ratified 2026-09-14 (calef, working the unratified worklist). Minted by milestone 78's lane
# on 2026-08-17. It borrows QEMU's own flag, `-icount`, and it is a noun (an instruction count).
#
# **Kept on the interface string, not on brevity, because calef challenged the brevity argument and
# it did not survive.** Asked directly why not `instruction-count`, two things were measured. The
# tree has no convention to appeal to: `script/` splits 29 hyphenated to 26 single-word, so
# `bench`, `lint`, `fmt`, `verify` and `soak` are as normal as `boot-check`, and "shorter is what we
# do" is not a fact about this tree. (That count is as it stood on 2026-09-14 when calef ruled, and
# it is left as measured. `soak` became `soak-test` later the same day, milestone 297, which moves
# the split to 30 and 25 and costs this sentence one of its five examples. The argument is
# unaffected: four single-word entry points still outnumber nothing, and the deciding reason below
# never rested on the count.) What decided it is that `icount` is load-bearing in at least
# five places that are not prose: QEMU's `-icount shift=0,sleep=off`, the cargo feature
# `--features icount`, `feature = "icount"` in both ISAs' `timer.rs` and in `bench.rs`, and the CI
# check named `bench (icount regression tripwire)`. **QEMU's flag is not ours to rename.** So
# spelling the script out gives one thing three spellings across a boundary we do not control,
# which is the split state milestone 265 spent 424 files ending for `_proto`.
#
# **The expansion does teach, and that is conceded rather than denied.** "Instruction count" says
# what is counted, which is the `dma` case rather than the `pci` case. The 2026-09-05 rule does not
# reach it only because `icount` is a contraction and not an acronym, the same distinction that kept
# `rm` on the same day. That escape was used three times in one session (`rm`, `virtio`, `icount`),
# which is frequent enough to be worth noticing: if it is too convenient a hatch, the fix is to
# tighten the rule rather than to decide one name against it.
#
# Considered and set aside: `instruction-clock`, which is arguably *more* accurate, since the
# mechanism is a virtual clock advancing one nanosecond per instruction retired rather than a count
# a reader consumes. It loses on the same ground `instruction-count` does: it is not the string the
# tool prints and accepts.
#
# **What ratifying the short name costs, so nobody rediscovers it**: a newcomer who has never run
# QEMU meets a word that does not say what it does, and the only thing that teaches them is this
# block and the usage line above.

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

# This project's own QEMU, if `script/ci-qemu` has built one (helpers/qemu-path.sh says why
# PATH is the mechanism). A no-op on macOS and on any machine without that prefix.
. helpers/qemu-path.sh
exec cargo xtask icount "$@"
